#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Substitution Effect]
#v(40pt)
If the price of good X decreases and the total quantity demanded increases, the substitution effect will \_\_\_\_\_.
#v(40pt)
// From the start until section one, the option is black. From section 2 until the end it is then turned green, because it is correct. 
#only("-1")[a) always be positive]#only("2-")[#text(fill:green)[a) always be positive]]
#v(10pt)
// From the start until section two, the option is black. From section 3 until the end it is then turned red, because it is false.
#only("-2")[b) always be negative]#only("3-")[#text(fill:red)[b) always be negative]]
#v(10pt)
// From the start until section three, the option is black. From section 4 until the end it is then turned red, because it is false.
#only("-3")[c) be zero]#only("4-")[#text(fill:red)[c) be zero]]
#v(10pt)
// From the start until section four, the option is black. From section 5 until the end it is then turned red, because it is false.
#only("-4")[d) be undefined]#only("5-")[#text(fill:red)[d) be undefined]]
#v(40pt)

#only("1")[#voiceover("Great job! That's the correct answer.")]
// Very briefly talk about a and why it is correct
#only("2")[#voiceover("The substitution effect will always be positive when the price decreases and total quantity demanded increases. Let's see why...")]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Substitution Effect]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[- Occurs when relative prices change 💸]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Holding utility constant 📊]
#v(20pt) 
#only("3-")[- Lower price $arrow$ relatively cheaper $arrow$ ↑ quantity demanded]
#v(20pt)
#only("4-")[- ∴ Substitution effect is positive when price ↓ and quantity ↑]

#only("1")[#voiceover("The substitution effect occurs when the relative prices of goods change, causing consumers to substitute towards the relatively cheaper good.")]
#only("2")[#voiceover("Importantly, the substitution effect is measured while holding the consumer's utility, or satisfaction, constant.")]
#only("3")[#voiceover("When the price of a good decreases, it becomes relatively cheaper compared to other goods. This leads to an increase in the quantity demanded of that good, as consumers substitute towards it.")]
#only("4")[#voiceover("Therefore, when the price decreases and the total quantity demanded increases, the substitution effect is always positive.")]
]

#slide()[
#text(size: 30pt, weight: "bold")[Why Other Options are Incorrect]
#v(40pt)
#only("1-")[b) always be negative ❌]
#v(20pt)
#only("2-")[- Substitution effect is positive when price ↓ and quantity ↑]
#v(40pt)
#only("3-")[c) be zero ❌]
#v(20pt)
#only("4-")[- Zero substitution effect means no change in relative prices]
#v(40pt) 
#only("5-")[d) be undefined ❌]
#v(20pt)
#only("6-")[- Substitution effect is always defined when prices and quantities change]

#only("1")[#voiceover("Option b, 'always be negative', is incorrect because the substitution effect is positive, not negative, when the price decreases and quantity increases.")]
#only("2")[#voiceover("As we saw, the substitution effect is positive in this scenario.")]
#only("3")[#voiceover("Option c, 'be zero', is also incorrect.")]
#only("4")[#voiceover("A zero substitution effect would mean there is no change in relative prices and thus no substitution between goods. But we are told the price of good X decreases, so there is a change in relative prices.")]
#only("5")[#voiceover("Finally, option d, 'be undefined', is incorrect as well.")]
#only("6")[#voiceover("The substitution effect is always defined when there are changes in prices and quantities. It's a fundamental concept in consumer theory.")]
]

